.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt GETRANDOM 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm getrandom
.Nd read bytes from the kernel random generator
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/random.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn getrandom "void *buf" "size_t buflen" "unsigned flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn getrandom
writes up to
.Fa buflen
bytes into
.Fa buf
from the kernel's ChaCha20 cryptographic pseudorandom number generator.
The i386 system call number is 355, and the x86-64 one 318.
musl uses this call for
.Fn getrandom .
.Pp
The generator is seeded once at boot.
The kernel first tries RDSEED, then RDRAND.
A complete key from either instruction is treated as trusted entropy.
If neither supplies a complete key, the kernel uses an untrusted seed
derived from the timestamp counter, CMOS seconds, and the PIT tick.
There is no later entropy accumulation or automatic rekeying.
.Pp
The boot record identifies the source as
.Ql entropy: ready: RDSEED, trusted ,
.Ql entropy: ready: RDRAND, trusted ,
or
.Ql entropy: ready: jitter, best-effort; the crypto RNG fails closed .
The last form does not indicate a trusted source.
.Pp
The
.Fa flags
argument is a bitwise OR of zero or more of:
.Bl -tag -width "GRND_NONBLOCK"
.It Dv GRND_NONBLOCK
Accepted with no effect.
The call never blocks waiting for entropy.
.It Dv GRND_RANDOM
Accepted with no effect.
There is only one random byte stream.
.It Dv GRND_INSECURE
Permit bytes from the untrusted fallback seed.
Without this flag, a nonempty request fails with
.Er EIO
when the source is untrusted.
.El
.Pp
Unknown flag bits and lengths greater than 2147483647 are rejected.
A zero-length request returns 0 after flag validation, without checking
the buffer or the entropy source.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn getrandom
returns the number of bytes written.
If a user-memory copy fails after some bytes have been written, it
returns that partial count.
On failure before any bytes have been written, it returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/timeprobe.c
makes two 16-byte requests with flags 0, requires each to return 16,
and checks that the two buffers differ.
This example requires a trusted entropy source.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A nonempty request has a null buffer, or the first copy to user memory fails.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
An unknown flag bit is set, or
.Fa buflen
exceeds 2147483647.
.It Bq Er EIO
The entropy source is untrusted and
.Dv GRND_INSECURE
was not specified.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr random 4
